+## Effect Rows (Type-Level)
+
+An **effect row** describes what a callable may *do*: `{FsRead}`, `{NetConnect,
+FsWrite}`, or `{}` for nothing at all. Rows are part of the function *type*,
+not part of the permission system described above -- and the distinction is
+load-bearing. A row is a claim about behaviour that the compiler checks. A
+permission is authority the *host* grants. Proving that a callback only reads
+files does not give it the right to read one, and holding `FsRead` does not
+make a function's declared row wider. They are separate facts and neither
+substitutes for the other.
+
+### Rows in type position
+
+A function type carries its row after the return type:
+
+```shape runnable=false
+fn(string) -> string ! {FsRead}    // may read files
+fn() -> int ! {}                   // does nothing observable
+```
+
+Subsumption is subset: a value whose row is `E1` fits where `E2` is expected
+exactly when `E1` is a subset of `E2`. A pure callback is therefore accepted
+anywhere an effectful one is, and never the reverse. Two function types that
+differ only in their row are different types.
+
+```shape
+fn twice(f: fn() -> int ! {}) -> int {
+    return f() + f()
+}
+
+print(twice(|| 21))
+```
+
+Generic code binds rows with an `effect` parameter, which closes to a concrete
+row at each instantiation:
+
+```shape runnable=false
+fn apply<T, effect F>(f: fn() -> T ! F) -> T ! F {
+    return f()
+}
+```
+
+### What is checked today, and what is not
+
+Shape is candid about the boundary here, because an effect system that is
+believed to be enforced but is not is worse than none at all.
+
+**Live now.** The row is a component of the function type. It participates in
+type identity and unification; the subset judgment is implemented in the
+constraint solver; and `effect` binders substitute to closed rows at
+instantiation. Ask the solver whether one row fits another and you get the
+right answer.
+
+**Not live yet.** Two things. Shape does not yet walk a function *body* to
+derive what it actually does, so bodies have no inferred row to compare a
+declaration against. And no production checking path asks the solver the
+subset question yet, so a row written in *type* position is recorded but not
+enforced: passing a `! {FsRead}` function where `! {}` is declared compiles
+today. Write rows in type position to document a boundary, not to rely on
+one.
+
+That gap is why an effect clause on a function **declaration** is currently a
+compile error:
+
+```shape runnable=false
+fn sneaky(path: string) -> string ! {} {
+    return file::read_text(path)     // reads a file
+}
+```
+
+```
+[C0934] the effect clause `! {}` on `fn sneaky` is not enforced yet, so it
+cannot be declared here.
+help: delete the `! {}` clause from `fn sneaky`. That preserves today's
+semantics exactly -- nothing was checking the row, so removing it removes no
+guarantee.
+```
+
+Before this rejection existed, that program compiled and ran, happily printing
+the file it had just declared it would not read. Rather than ship a contract
+that can lie, Shape refuses the declaration until the body walk lands -- the
+same call the language made for `async` foreign declarations. Rows in *type*
+position are unaffected: they are recorded rather than claimed, so writing one
+asserts nothing about a body that nothing has read.
+
+:::note[Arriving in a later release]
+Declaration-position rows, body row inference, enforcement of type-position
+subsumption, and the resulting declaration-vs-body checking are planned work,
+tracked as EFFECT-CONTRACT. When it lands, `[C0934]` disappears and a declared
+row becomes a checked promise: a body that exceeds it fails with the excess
+effects named, and the compiler offers the corrected row as a one-keystroke
+fix.
+:::
+
